 Step-by-step instructions for the beginner
The wooden chair you've inherited or bought at a thrift store is an heirloom that's been in your family for generations, but after years of use, it's starting to show its age. The legs are loose, the joints are creaky, and the finish is worn off. Don't worry; with patience and the right techniques, you can restore your wooden chair to its former glory. In this guide, we'll walk you through the steps to repair a wooden chair, from assessing the damage to applying the finishing touches.
Step 1: Assess the Damage
Before you start the repair process, it's essential to assess the extent of the damage. Take a close look at the chair and make a mental or written note of the following:
• Loose joints: Are the joints loose and creaky? Are they coming apart?
• Loose legs: Are the legs loose and wobbly? Are they detached from the seat or backrest?
